{
  "id": "proj-forged-bottle-opener",
  "slug": "forged-bottle-opener",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Forged Bottle Opener",
  "summary": "Drawn out and twisted from mild steel bar stock. Your first hour at an anvil, with something to show for it.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 3,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Set up outside or somewhere with real ventilation (a propane forge gives off carbon monoxide). Put the anvil face at knuckle height, a bucket of water close by, and the vise on a solid bench.",
    "Hacksaw a 10 in length from the bar. Use only bare, uncoated steel: galvanized steel gives off zinc fumes when heated.",
    "Heat the first 2 1/2 in to orange and flatten it into a paddle about 3/16 in thick and 1 1/4 in wide. Flip it every few blows so it stays centred.",
    "Punch the hole: at a bright orange heat, drive the punch into the middle of the paddle until a dark spot shows on the back, flip it, and knock the slug out over the anvil's pritchel or hardy hole. Cool the punch in water often.",
    "Drift the hole out to about 3/4 in, leaving at least 1/4 in of metal all round, then flatten the paddle again where the drift made it bulge.",
    "Heat the other end, taper it over about 3 in, and scroll it into a loop over the horn so the opener can hang on a hook.",
    "Heat the middle 3 in evenly, clamp the bar in the vise at the edge of the hot zone, and twist it one or two full turns with the wrench. Straighten it on the anvil with light blows.",
    "Try it on a bottle cap: the edge of the cap should catch under the near lip of the hole. Wire-brush off the scale, warm the opener until beeswax melts and smokes on it, rub the wax in, and wipe off the excess."
  ],
